    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-16 lg:py-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic border-l-4 border-[#38BDF8] pl-6 bg-gray-50 py-4 rounded-r-xl">
          The short version: spackle fills holes, joint compound covers seams. Spackle is faster and shrinks less; joint compound spreads thinner and feathers out to nothing. Almost every wall repair you will ever do has an obvious answer once you know which of those two things you are doing.
        </p>

        <h2 id="what-they-are" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          1. What Each One Actually Is
        </h2>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Joint Compound</h3>
        <p>
          Joint compound — universally called mud on a job site — is a gypsum-based paste designed for finishing drywall. Its job is to bed paper or mesh tape over the seams between boards and then be feathered out in successive thin coats until the joint is invisible under paint. It is sold in large tubs and buckets because drywall finishing consumes it by the gallon, and it is deliberately loose enough to spread over a wide area with a broad knife.
        </p>
        <p>
          Two consequences follow from that design. It shrinks noticeably as the water leaves it, which is why finishing is done in two or three coats rather than one. And it dries slowly — 12 to 24 hours per coat is normal, longer in a cold or humid room.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Spackle</h3>
        <p>
          Spackle is a thicker vinyl or acrylic paste built for a different problem: filling a small hole or dent in one application and being sandable quickly. It holds its shape in a nail hole rather than slumping, shrinks very little, and is often ready to sand within an hour. It is sold in small tubs because that is all any repair of this type needs.
        </p>
        <p>
          Its limitations follow just as directly. It is too thick and sets too fast to spread thinly over a wide area, and it has no business anywhere near drywall tape. Our <a href="/spackle-vs-putty/">spackle vs wood putty guide</a> covers where it sits against the wood-repair products it is also confused with.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Cost and Shelf Life</h3>
        <p>
          Neither material is expensive, and the sensible way to think about the money is waste rather than price per tub. A small container of spackle costs a few dollars and, kept sealed, stays usable for years — it is one of the few products where buying the smallest size is genuinely correct. Joint compound is cheap by volume and expensive by wastage: a five-gallon bucket bought for a single patch will skin over, dry out and go to the tip almost full. Buy the smallest tub that covers the job, press cling film onto the surface before closing the lid, and it will keep for months rather than weeks.
        </p>

        <!-- Head-to-Head Comparison Table -->
        <div class="my-16 bg-gradient-to-br from-gray-950 to-gray-900 rounded-3xl p-8 lg:p-12 text-white shadow-2xl">
          <h2 id="head-to-head" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-white mt-0 mb-8 border-none text-center">Head-to-Head Comparison</h2>
          <div class="overflow-x-auto">
            <table class="w-full text-sm text-left border-collapse">
