                  <td class="py-3 px-4 text-green-400">Seams, tape, skim coats</td>
                  <td class="py-3 px-4 text-green-400">Nail holes, dents, small dings</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-800">
                  <td class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold text-white">Dry time per coat</td>
                  <td class="py-3 px-4">12-24 hours</td>
                  <td class="py-3 px-4 text-green-400">30 minutes to a few hours</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-800">
                  <td class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold text-white">Shrinkage</td>
                  <td class="py-3 px-4 text-yellow-400">Noticeable — plan on coats</td>
                  <td class="py-3 px-4 text-green-400">Minimal</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-800">
                  <td class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold text-white">Spreads thin?</td>
                  <td class="py-3 px-4 text-green-400">Yes, feathers to nothing</td>
                  <td class="py-3 px-4 text-red-400">No, too thick</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-800">
                  <td class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold text-white">Works with tape?</td>
                  <td class="py-3 px-4 text-green-400">Yes — that is the point</td>
                  <td class="py-3 px-4 text-red-400">No</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-800">
                  <td class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold text-white">Sold in</td>
                  <td class="py-3 px-4">Gallons and buckets</td>
                  <td class="py-3 px-4">Small tubs and tubes</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold text-white">Typical job length</td>
                  <td class="py-3 px-4">Two to three days</td>
                  <td class="py-3 px-4 text-green-400">One afternoon</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2 id="types-of-compound" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          3. The Types of Joint Compound
        </h2>

        <p>
          Four kinds sit on the shelf, and picking wrongly makes the job harder rather than impossible.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">All-Purpose</h3>
        <p>
          The default, and the right choice if you are buying one bucket. It bonds well enough to embed tape and finishes smoothly enough for the final coat. Slightly harder to sand than lightweight, and slightly stronger — a fair trade for most DIY work.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Lightweight (All-Purpose)</h3>
        <p>
          Same role, less dense, and considerably easier to sand. It is the friendlier option for anyone finishing a whole room by hand, at the cost of a little strength. Many finishers use standard for the bedding coat and lightweight for the finish coats.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Topping</h3>
        <p>
          Formulated purely for the final coat: very smooth, low shrinkage, easy to sand, and not strong enough to bed tape. Use it only over already-taped joints. If you are not finishing a full room, you do not need it.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Setting-Type ("Hot Mud")</h3>
        <p>
          Sold as a powder you mix with water, it cures chemically on a fixed clock rather than drying by evaporation — 20, 45 and 90 minute versions are common. That makes it the answer for deep fills, big patches, and any job where you want a second coat the same day. It is also very hard once set, so plan to shape it before it cures rather than sanding it afterwards. For anything deeper than about half an inch, this is the correct material.
        </p>

        <p>
          One practical note on mixing setting-type compound: it starts curing the moment water goes in, so mix only what you can use in the working window on the bag, and mix it in a clean bucket. A trace of already-cured material left in the tub acts as a seed and accelerates the whole batch, which is how a supposedly forty-five minute mix goes hard in fifteen.
        </p>

        <h2 id="types-of-spackle" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          4. The Types of Spackle
        </h2>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Lightweight Spackle</h3>
        <p>
          Whipped, almost mousse-like, and the fastest thing available for nail holes. It barely shrinks, sands with a fingernail, and dries in half an hour. Its weakness is durability: it is soft, so it does not belong anywhere that gets knocked, and it does not hold a screw or a hook.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Standard Vinyl Spackle</h3>
        <p>
          Denser and tougher, suitable for slightly larger dents and for surfaces that will see contact — a hallway, a stairwell, a kitchen wall. It takes longer to dry and sands a little harder, and in deeper fills it shrinks slightly, so a second pass is sometimes needed.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Epoxy and Two-Part Fillers</h3>
        <p>
          Rigid, waterproof and far stronger than either paste, these are for exterior repairs and for damage in wood or masonry rather than drywall. They are overkill indoors and unpleasant to sand, but nothing else fills a hole in an exterior door frame as durably.
        </p>

        <h2 id="by-job" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          5. Which One, By Job
        </h2>

        <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
          <table class="w-full text-left">
            <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
              <tr>
                <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">The Job</th>
                <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Use</th>
                <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Why</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Nail and screw holes</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Lightweight spackle</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">One pass, sandable in an hour</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Anchor and hook holes</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Standard spackle</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Deeper, needs some body</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Drywall seams and corners</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Joint compound + tape</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Only mud feathers out far enough</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Doorknob hole in drywall</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Patch + joint compound</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Needs backing, not just filler</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Deep gouge over 1/2 inch</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Setting-type compound</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Builds thick without slumping</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Skim coating a whole wall</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Joint compound</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Spreadable over large areas</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Hairline crack in plaster</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Mesh tape + compound</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Filler alone re-cracks</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-gray-50">
                <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Exterior trim damage</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Epoxy filler</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Neither paste survives weather</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <p>
          The one row worth dwelling on is the plaster crack. Filling a crack with any paste and painting over it feels finished and almost always fails, because the crack is the result of movement that has not stopped. Bridging it with self-adhesive mesh tape and then covering the tape with compound is what actually holds, and it is barely more work.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">The Three Repairs You Will Actually Do</h3>
        <p>
          <strong>A nail hole.</strong> Press the tip of a putty knife around the rim first to knock down the raised paper ridge that a nail leaves behind — skip this and the patch will always sit proud. Push lightweight spackle in with the knife held almost flat, scrape the surface clean in one pass, and leave it. Sand lightly in an hour, prime, paint. The whole repair takes about two minutes of actual work.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>An anchor hole.</strong> Plastic anchors leave a hole with a torn, flared rim that filler alone cannot bridge. Tap the anchor through into the cavity with a screwdriver, or cut it flush, then press the torn paper back down. Fill with standard vinyl spackle in two passes rather than one deep push, letting the first dry so the second has something to sit on.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>A doorknob hole.</strong> This is not a filling job at all. Anything more than about two inches across needs backing behind it — a self-adhesive mesh patch for small holes, or a cut piece of drywall with a wood strip screwed behind it for larger ones. Then tape the edges, bed them in joint compound, and feather out in two or three coats. No amount of filler spanning empty air will hold.
        </p>

        <h2 id="finishing" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          6. Sanding and Painting
        </h2>

        <p>
          Both materials sand, and the grit matters more than the product. Anything coarser than 150 grit leaves scratches that show through paint; 180 to 220 is the working range for a finish coat. Sand with a light touch — the aim is to blend the edge of the patch into the wall, not to remove material — and a sanding sponge gives better control on small repairs than a block.
        </p>

        <p>
          On a whole room, dust becomes the problem rather than the sanding. A drywall sander with dust extraction turns an unpleasant day into a manageable one, and our <a href="/best-drywall-sander/">drywall sander guide</a> covers the options. Wiping down with a damp sponge instead of sanding also works on small patches and produces no dust at all.
        </p>

        <p>
          Then prime the patch. Both spackle and compound are more porous than the surrounding paint, so painting straight over a repair leaves a dull patch with a different sheen — flashing — that is especially obvious on satin and semi-gloss walls. A dab of primer takes seconds and is the difference between a repair you cannot find and one you notice every time the light is right.
        </p>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">Common Mistakes</h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Filling a Seam With Spackle</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A drywall seam needs tape and a compound that feathers out over ten inches or more. Spackle can do neither, and the repair cracks along its length in the first heating season.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">One Thick Coat of Compound</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Joint compound shrinks as it dries, so a thick coat sinks in the middle and cracks. Two or three thin coats, each dried fully, get there faster than one heavy one you have to repair.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sanding Before It Has Dried Through</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Compound that looks dry on the surface can still be damp underneath. Sanding it gums the paper, drags the patch out of the hole, and leaves a hollow you then have to fill again. Wait the full time on the tub.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping Primer on the Patch</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Both materials drink paint differently from the wall around them. Without primer the repair shows as a duller patch under any sheen above flat, and no amount of extra topcoat fixes it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using Lightweight Spackle Where Things Get Knocked</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                It is soft by design. In a hallway, a stairwell or behind a door handle, standard vinyl spackle or setting-type compound holds up; lightweight will dent again the first time something brushes past.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Verdict -->
        <div id="verdict" class="my-16 bg-gradient-to-br from-gray-950 to-gray-900 rounded-3xl p-8 lg:p-12 text-white shadow-2xl relative overflow-hidden">
          <div class="absolute top-0 right-0 bg-[#FF4500] text-white font-black text-xs uppercase tracking-widest px-4 py-2 rounded-bl-xl">
            ★ Our Verdict
          </div>
          <h2 id="who-wins" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-white mt-0 mb-6 border-none">Joint Compound vs Spackle: Who Wins?</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6 text-gray-300">
            <p>
              <strong class="text-[#FF4500]">Reach for spackle if:</strong> you are filling holes. Nail holes, anchor holes, small dents and dings — anything where you want it done, sanded and painted this afternoon. A small tub costs very little and lasts years.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong class="text-[#38BDF8]">Reach for joint compound if:</strong> you are covering area. Seams, corners, tape, patches with backing behind them, skim coats, and anything that needs to disappear into the wall over ten inches rather than fill a hole an inch across.
            </p>
            <p class="text-lg border-t border-gray-700 pt-6 mt-6">
              <strong class="text-white">The Honest Bottom Line:</strong> Own both — a small tub of lightweight spackle for everyday holes and a bucket of all-purpose compound for anything structural. If you can only have one, all-purpose joint compound does more jobs, just more slowly. And whichever you use, sand at 180 to 220 grit and prime the patch: the repair itself is rarely what gives a bad wall repair away.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <h2 id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          Frequently Asked Questions
        </h2>

        <div class="space-y-6">
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between joint compound and spackle?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Joint compound is a gypsum mud for spreading over drywall seams with tape and feathering out in wide thin coats. Spackle is a thicker, faster-drying vinyl or acrylic paste for filling small holes in one pass with minimal shrinkage. Compound covers area; spackle fills volume.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use joint compound instead of spackle?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Yes for small holes, with two caveats: it shrinks, so a deep hole often needs a second pass, and it dries far more slowly. If a bucket is already open for a bigger job, using it on nail holes makes sense. Buying one just for four picture hooks does not.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use spackle on drywall seams?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">No. Seams need tape bedded in compound and feathered over 10 to 12 inches, and spackle is too thick and sets too fast to be worked that way. A taped-free seam filled with spackle cracks along its length, usually within the first heating season.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Which dries faster?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Spackle, comfortably — lightweight versions are often sandable within the hour. All-purpose joint compound wants 12 to 24 hours per coat, longer when it is cold or humid. The exception is setting-type compound, which cures chemically in 20, 45 or 90 minutes depending on the bag.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Does spackle shrink?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Lightweight spackle barely shrinks, which is why it fills a nail hole in one application. Standard vinyl shrinks a little in deeper fills. Joint compound shrinks noticeably more, which is exactly why drywall finishing is done in multiple thin coats.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Do you have to prime before painting?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Yes on anything with a sheen. Both materials are more porous than the surrounding paint, so a patch painted directly dries to a different lustre and shows as a dull spot. A quick coat of primer on the repair prevents that flashing entirely.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">What should I use for a deep hole?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Setting-type joint compound for anything over about half an inch deep — it cures chemically and builds thick without slumping or cracking. For a hole more than a couple of inches across, use a drywall patch or new board, taped and finished with compound.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Which is easier to sand?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Lightweight spackle and lightweight compound both sand very easily. Standard all-purpose is more work but finishes finer. Setting-type compound is hardest once cured, which is why it is used for fill coats rather than the final one — shape it before it sets rather than sanding it after.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

