        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">Spackle fills, joint compound covers.</strong> A nail hole wants a small tub of lightweight spackle and an hour. A seam, a corner, a patch or a skim coat wants a bucket of all-purpose compound, tape, and a couple of days of thin coats with drying time between them.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
            When the fill is deeper than half an inch, reach past both for setting-type compound. When the wall is cracked rather than holed, put mesh tape over it first, whatever you fill it with. And prime every patch before painting — the sheen difference, not the filler, is what makes an amateur repair obvious across a room.
          </p>
        </div>

